      "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in dayofanyweek."
    },
    "n_outputRateRow": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 684,
      "description": "THE ONE DECLARED NUMBER IN THIS DOCUMENT: how many (pollutant-process, cohort) pairs reach MOVESOutput, over all six pollutant-processes together. 684 = 3 x 124 exhaust + 3 x 104 crankcase, and it is ONE number rather than a per-block cohort count BECAUSE THE BLOCKS ARE NOT THE SAME SIZE -- the first fixture in this port where they are not. `process-tirewear` could declare `n_outputCohort` and multiply, because every one of its pollutant-processes spanned the same 125 cohorts; here the twenty electricity cohorts have no crankcaseEmissionRatio row, MOVES's join is an inner one, and the three crankcase blocks are twenty cohorts shorter than the three exhaust ones. So the rank is GLOBAL over the rate relation (docs/esm-conventions.md 31): `rt_outputRank` numbers the emitting rate rows 1..684 by an inclusive prefix count and the output row joins to that rank, which needs no block to be rectangular. WHICH rows emit is a result of the chain and is never written down; `run_outputRateRowCount` recomputes 684 from it and `pp_emittedCohortCount` recomputes 124/104 per pollutant-process."
    },
    "n_runspecpollutantprocess": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in runspecpollutantprocess. The FIRST factor of both the rate relation and the output relation: three pollutant-processes here, and a run selecting one more would size both relations differently with no other edit."
    },
    "n_runspecchainedto": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in runspecchainedto."
    },
    "n_crankcaseemissionratio": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 0,
      "description": "Rows in crankcaseemissionratio."
    }
  },
